   and Conway, Paul P.},
Title = {A review of design frameworks for human-cyber-physical systems moving
   from industry 4 to 5},
Journal = {IET CYBER-PHYSICAL SYSTEMS: THEORY \& APPLICATIONS},
Year = {2024},
Volume = {9},
Number = {2},
Pages = {169-183},
Month = {JUN},
Abstract = {Within the Industry 4.0 landscape, humans collaborate with cyber and
   physical elements to form human-cyber-physical systems (HCPS). These
   environments are increasingly complex and challenging workspaces due to
   increasing levels of automation and data availability. An effective
   system design requires suitable frameworks that consider human
   activities and needs whilst supporting overall system efficacy. Although
   several reviews of frameworks for technology were identified, none of
   these focused on the human in the system (moving towards Industry 5).
   The critical literature review presented provides a summary of HCPS
   frameworks, maps the considerations for a human in HCPS, and provides
   insight for future framework and system development. The challenges,
   recommendations, and areas for further research are discussed.
   Effective system design moving towards industry 5.0 requires suitable
   frameworks that consider human activities and needs whilst supporting
   overall system efficacy. The critical literature review presented in
   this paper provides a summary of HCPS frameworks, maps the
   considerations for a human in HCPS and provides insight for future
   framework and system development. The challenges, recommendations, and
   areas for further research are discussed.image},
